enix, Arizona, where they attended an underfunded public high school. No one had ever suggested to Oscar, Cristian, Luis, or Lorenzo that they might amount to much—but two inspiring science teachers had convinced these impoverished, undocumented kids from the desert who had never even seen the ocean that they should try to build an underwater robot. And build a robot they did. Their robot wasn't pretty, especially compared to those of the competition. They were going up against some of the best collegiate engineers in the country, including a team from MIT backed by a $10,000 grant from ExxonMobil. The Phoenix teenagers had scraped together less than $1,000 and built their robot out of scavenged parts. This was never a level competition—and yet, against all odds . . . they won!","subjectCategories":[{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","label":"Biography & Autobiography","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-spare-parts-four-undocumented-teenagers-one-ugly-robot-and-the-battle-for-the-american-dream-jos"}],"displaySummary":"The Phoenix teenagers had scraped together less than $1,000 and built their robot out of scavenged parts. This was never a level competition—and yet, against all odds . . . they won!","primaryTopic":"Immigration, Refugees & Borderlands","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Race & Ethnicity","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Race & Ethnicity","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"Race & Ethnicity","mappedSubject":"Race & Ethnicity","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book examines the experiences of undocumented Latino teenagers and their cultural struggles, while also highlighting their achievement in a technology competition.","id":"subject-evidence-book-spare-parts-four-undocumented-teenagers-one-ugly-robot-and-the-battle-for-the-american-dream-jos-1"},{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","rawLabel":"Biography & A